2026-11-12T15:13:54Z	browser	pass	cd /Users/rafiulislam/Work/n8n/OHM/website && npx eslint components app lib 2>&1 | tail -3; npm run build 2>&1 | grep -E "Compiled successfully|Turbopack build failed" | head -1; git add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'MSG' && git log --oneline -1 Dashboard: say something while a screen is loading, and when one fails  Measured first. Clicking Overview left the browser on the previous page for 1.9 seconds with nothing happening: Campaigns 1.2s, Channels 1.0s. Every screen is a server component reading Convex, and Calls reads Retell as well, so on the live deployment those are network hops rather than localhost. There were no loading states and no error boundaries across twelve pages.  Now the rail, the eyebrow and the page title appear in about 150ms, because they are known before any data is, and the content area carries placeholders shaped like what is coming. A skeleton that is the wrong size is worse than a spinner, so the blocks hold the real grid and nothing jumps when the data lands. aria-busy and one polite status line tell a screen reader the page is still arriving; the forty grey rectangles are hidden from it, because announcing them one by one is noise.  Two things the screenshots caught that the markup did not.  The first version used rgb(var(--ink-rgb)/.06). There is no --ink-rgb in this project, so the colour resolved to nothing and every placeholder rendered invisible: the screen looked empty, not loading. It is bg-line now, which is a real token, defined for both themes, and already used across the dashboard. Checked in dark mode too: 51 points of luminance against the page, where 0 would be invisible again.  The second version drew each table row as one full-width bar with one-pixel gaps, and on screen that read as a grey slab rather than as a table. Rows are cells of varying width now, which is what makes a skeleton read as text.  error.tsx keeps a failed screen inside the product: what happened, a Try again, a way out, and the digest to quote if they ring about it. It does not guess at the cause, because being told the wrong reason is worse than being told none. It carries no rail, unlike the loading state: this file is a client component and the rail lives in Shell.tsx, which reads cookies, so importing it pulls server-only code into the browser bundle and the build refuses. Two links out rather than a second copy of the rail that would drift.  941 assertions, tsc, eslint and build clean, every screen still clean at 1440 and 390.  Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com> MSG	5723cc8 Dashboard: say something while a screen is loading, and when one fails
2026-11-12T15:16:59Z	build	pass	npm run build 2>&1 | grep -E "Compiled successfully|Turbopack build failed" | head -1; cd widget && zsh tests/run.sh 2>&1 | grep -E "every suite|SOME" | tail -1; cd .. && git add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'MSG' && git log --oneline -1 Fix a crash on any keydown that carries no key  Reported from the browser: "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toLowerCase')" at SiteSearch.tsx:29, taking the whole page down with the Next.js error overlay.  `key` is optional on KeyboardEvent and genuinely arrives undefined. Password managers and browser autofill dispatch synthetic keydowns without it, which is why this surfaced around a sign-in rather than anywhere somebody was really typing. SiteSearch listens on `window`, so it sees every one of those events and had to survive them.  Two places made the same assumption and both are guarded now. The other 45 uses of event.key in this codebase compare with === , which is false against undefined and was never at risk.  Verified by dispatching a keydown with key undefined, which previously threw and now does nothing, and by pressing Cmd+K afterwards to confirm the search still opens.  Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com> MSG git fetch origin -q && T=ghp_REDACTED && git push "https://onlinemarketinghelpuk:${T}@github.com/onlinemarketinghelpuk/omh-redesign.git" main 2>&1 | sed "s/${T}/***/g" | tail -2	5723cc8..98fbea3  main -> main
